procstep.procdesc:
The first step in our GEE code involved uploading a shapefile of polygons representing each fire in our dataset as an asset within GEE. This shapefile and associated metadata can be downloaded from ScienceBase here: https://doi.org/10.5066/P9BB5TIO

Each fire perimeter polygon within the shapefile is named for the fire that it represents, and each dNBR in this database is named accordingly after each fire.

Our code then goes on to call the pre and post-fire Landsat images which it then uses to compute a pre-fire NBR and a post-fire NBR. The two NBRs are then differenced to calculate the dNBR.   

The code then specifies that the final dNBR be clipped according to the boundaries of the fire using the polygon geometry from the shapefile, and outputs the final result as a 30 meter 8-bit image.

What follows is a brief version of sample code used to create a dNBR, our example fire is named - 
"LOS-AJOS-LA-MADERA-20130618":

// define the individual fire poly from the shapefile
var fire1 = FirePolys.filterMetadata('Fire_ID','equals',"LOS-AJOS-LA-MADERA-20130618");

// Load two Landsat 5 images.
var imagePRE = ee.Image('LANDSAT/LE07/C01/T1/LE07_035039_20130612');
var imagePOST = ee.Image('LANDSAT/LE07/C01/T1/LE07_035039_20140701');

// Compute pre-fire NBR.
var nbrPRE = imagePRE.normalizedDifference(['B5', 'B7']);
// Compute post-fire NBR.
var nbrPOST = imagePOST.normalizedDifference(['B5', 'B7']);
// Compute dNBR
var dNBR = ((nbrPRE.subtract(nbrPOST)).multiply(1000));

// Clip the dNBR to the polygon geometry
var dNBR_clip = dNBR.clip(fire1);

//color scale properties for dnbr
var dNBRViz = {min: -100, max: 779, palette: ['09f510','fff81d','ff0326']};

// Add a map layer of the dNBR clipped to the polygon.
Map.addLayer(dNBR_clip, dNBRViz, 'dNBR', true);

//Set id variable to the same Fire_ID
var id =  "LOS-AJOS-LA-MADERA-20130618"
;
Export.image.toDrive({
image: dNBR,
description: id,
folder:'dNBR',
scale: 30,
maxPixels: 10000000000000,
region: area
});
